```

This will start Cypress inside the Docker container, using the configured "app-image" and "docker" settings in the `cypress.json` file.

By following these steps, your Cypress testing framework will utilize Docker, take screenshots upon test failure as configured in the `cypress.json`, and execute tests within the specified Docker image.

Here's a C++ function that converts markdown to HTML formatting, taking a reference to a std::string containing markdown text and returning a std::string with the corresponding HTML:

```c++
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <regex>

std::string markdown_to_html(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a mapping of markdown syntax to HTML equivalents
    const std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> syntax_map = {
        {"#", "<h1>"},
        {"##", "<h2>"},
        {"###", "<h3>"},
        {"####", "<h4>"},
        {"#####", "<h5>"},
        {"######", "<h6>"},
        {"* ", "<li>"},
        {"`", "<code>"},
        {"\[ ", "<a>"},
        {"\(|https?://[^\s]+", "</a>"},
        {"[ ", "<a>"},
        {"| (.*?) \]", "</a><br>"},
        {"> ", "<blockquote>"}
    };

    // Function to replace all instances of markdown syntax with their HTML equivalents
    auto replace_syntax = [syntax_map](const std::string& input) -> std::string {
        std::regex syntax_regex("\\b(" + std::regex_replace(":;[]", "[^\\]\\[]", "\\") + ")\\b");  // Regex pattern for markdown syntax matches
        std::regex_replace_regex_options<> opts{std::regex_constants::format_all};
        std::smatch match;
        std::string output = input;
        while (std::regex_search(output, match, syntax_regex, opts)) {
            output.replace(match.position(), match.str().length(), syntax_map[match.str(1)]);
        }
        return output;
    };

    // Replace markdown syntax with their HTML equivalents and remove excess line breaks
    std::string html_text = replace_syntax(markdown);
    html_text = std::regex_replace(html_text, std::regex("\\n{2,}"), "\n\n");  // Convert multiple line breaks to a single newline

    return html_text;
}
```

This function uses a map to store common markdown syntax and their corresponding HTML tags. The `replace_syntax` function iterates through the markdown string, replaces the matched syntax with their HTML equivalents, and updates the resulting string. The final conversion to HTML is done by calling `replace_syntax` on the original markdown text. The function also removes excessive line breaks during the conversion process.

