2. Define FEN to LaTeX converter macros:
To process the FEN notation efficiently, create a few helper macros as follows:

	```latex
	% FEN to LaTeX converter macros
	\newcommand{\getpieces}[1]{\StrSubstitute{#1}{/}{\&}}
	\newcommand{\getboard}[1]{\StrSubuse{#1}{1}})
	\newcommand{\makecolumn}[3]{\IfEq{#1}{b}
	{#2} {#3}}
	\newcommand{\makeline}[3]{\IfEq{#1}{8}
	{#2} {#3}}
	```

3. Use the converter macros to generate board and pieces:
Now, use the `getpieces`, `getboard`, `makecolumn`, and `makeline` macros to create the board and place pieces based on the provided FEN notation. The following code illustrates how to do this for a typical FEN:

	```latex
	\begin{document}

	% Replace <FEN> with your FEN notation here
	\def\FEN{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR}

	% Get board height (number of rows) and width (number of columns)
	\def\boardwidth{\getboard{\FEN}}
	\def\boardheight{\StringLength{\boardwidth}}
